  {
    "id": "p-eeade6",
    "offset": 14981,
    "text": "Luke 22: 38."
  },
  {
    "id": "p-3fe95a",
    "offset": 14995,
    "text": "It has been objected that no proof is offered that social laws exist in the order of nature. By what demonstration could any such proof be given a priori? If a man of scientific training finds his attention arrested, in some group of phenomena, by these sequences, relations, and recurrences which he has learned to note as signs of action of law, he seeks to discover the law. If it exists, he finds it. What other proof of its existeaces could there be?"
  },
  {
    "id": "p-2dde17",
    "offset": 15452,
    "text": "Noted in Chapter VI of Sumner's What Social Classes Owe to Each Other."
  },
